      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2012 Elsevier Masson SAS. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Multislice computed tomography coronary angiography (MSCT-CA) is feasible in the emergency department (ED) for ruling out obstructive coronary artery disease (CAD)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIM",
            "content": "To investigate a diagnostic strategy using MSCT-CA for the early triage of patients presenting to the ED with acute chest pain suggestive of acute coronary syndrome (ACS), according to the medium-term incidence of clinical events."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We conducted a single-centre, prospective, observational cohort study in 123 patients with low-risk to intermediate-risk acute chest pain suggestive of ACS. MSCT-CA was performed using dual-source 64-slice computed tomography with retrospective electrocardiographic gating. Patients without coronary artery lesions were discharged from the ED. The incidences of death, myocardial infarction and myocardial revascularization were collected during a mid-term follow-up."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "According to MSCT-CA, 93 patients (75.6%) had no CAD or coronary artery stenosis less or equal to 50% and 28 patients (22.8%) had stenosis more or equal to 50%. Invasive coronary angiography was performed in 29 patients (23.6%). MSCT-CA accurately identified ten patients (8.13%) with obstructive CAD requiring myocardial revascularization; all had a low TIMI score (0-2) and eight had a low GRACE score. The mean estimated effective dose of MSCT-CA was 16.3±6.4 mSv. Median follow-up was 15 months. No patient (95% CI 0-3.0%) had major adverse cardiovascular events during follow-up."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "MSCT-CA appears to be a useful initial triage tool in the ED. When the MSCT-CA result is negative, it allows safe early discharge because of its high negative predictive value. In a significant number of cases of low-risk ACS, MSCT-CA detects severe coronary lesions and allows further dedicated diagnostic and therapeutic intervention. Reduction of radiation exposure would help acceptance in clinical practice."
          }
        ]
      },
